## ExampleCollector

Defined in `internal/collector/collector.go`. Replace this section's rows when
you adapt `ExampleCollector` into your real collector.

| Metric | Type | Labels | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `@@NAMESPACE@@_items` | Gauge | - | Number of items reported by the example target. |
| `@@NAMESPACE@@_healthy` | Gauge | - | Whether the example target reports itself healthy (1) or not (0). |

## Self-instrumentation

On a `single` build, each row below is gated by its own `--collector.<name>`
flag (`--collector.http_client_requests`, `--collector.http_client_request_wait`;
both default `true`, negate with `--no-collector.<name>`), the same as any
other collector. A `multi` build (which shares this file, see the `/probe`
section below) registers both unconditionally instead, with no such flag.
See [docs/configuration.md](configuration.md).

| Metric | Type | Labels | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `@@NAMESPACE@@_exporter_request_duration_seconds` | Histogram | `outcome` | Duration in seconds of HTTP requests issued by this exporter's collectors, by outcome (`success` or `error`). Defined in `internal/collector/client.go`. |
| `@@NAMESPACE@@_exporter_request_wait_seconds` | Histogram | `outcome` | Duration in seconds a request waited for a per-target concurrency slot, by outcome (`success` if a slot was granted, `error` if the caller's own deadline expired first). Both series stay at a permanent zero once `--exporter.max-requests-per-target` is unset (the default); a multi-target build never sets a ceiling, so both are a permanent zero there. Defined in `internal/collector/limiter.go`. |
| `@@NAMESPACE@@_exporter_collector_success` | Gauge | `collector` | Whether the last scrape of the collector succeeded (1=success, 0=failure). Defined in `internal/collector/status_tracker.go`. |
| `@@NAMESPACE@@_exporter_collector_duration_seconds` | Gauge | `collector` | Duration of the last scrape for the collector, in seconds. Defined in `internal/collector/status_tracker.go`. |

## Multi-target `/probe` metrics (opt-in builds only)

Multi-target builds (scaffolded with `--target-model multi`) additionally
expose two gauges on `/probe?target=…`, defined in `internal/probe/probe.go`,
a sibling package to `internal/collector/`, which does not exist at all in
a single-target build. They are listed here as prose rather than a
`docs-check`-parsed table row above on purpose: `make docs-check` (see this
file's own header comment) only scans `internal/collector/*.go`, so it can
never see `internal/probe`'s metrics either way: documenting them as a
regular table row would make `docs-check` report them as a lie for every
build, single and multi alike.

- `probe_success` (Gauge, no labels): `1` if every collector scoped to this
  probe reported a successful scrape, `0` otherwise.
- `probe_duration_seconds` (Gauge, no labels): wall-clock duration of the
  whole probe.

Both are deliberately **un-namespaced** (no `@@NAMESPACE@@_` prefix): every
multi-target exporter in the Prometheus ecosystem (Blackbox, SNMP) emits
these two exact names, and every multi-target dashboard/alert in the wild
queries them as-is. This scaffold matches that convention here instead of
the `namespace_subsystem_name` rule the rest of this file's metrics follow.

## Configuration reload metrics (multi-target builds only)

Multi-target builds (scaffolded with `--target-model multi`) additionally
expose two gauges, defined in `internal/reload/reload.go`, a sibling package
to `internal/collector/`, which does not exist at all in a single-target
build. They are listed here as prose rather than a `docs-check`-parsed table
row on purpose: `make docs-check` (see this file's own header comment) only
scans `internal/collector/*.go`, so it can never see `internal/reload`'s
metrics either way; documenting them as a regular table row would make
`docs-check` report them as a lie against a directory it was never told to
look at, on every build, single and multi alike.

- `@@NAMESPACE@@_exporter_config_last_reload_successful` (Gauge, no labels):
  whether the last configuration reload attempt succeeded (`1`) or failed
  (`0`). Set to `1` before the server starts serving, so the gauge is
  meaningful from the very first scrape rather than absent until somebody
  reloads.
- `@@NAMESPACE@@_exporter_config_last_reload_success_timestamp_seconds`
  (Gauge, no labels): Unix time of the last SUCCESSFUL configuration reload.

Reload is triggered by `SIGHUP` (always on) or `POST /-/reload` (behind
`--web.enable-lifecycle`); see [docs/configuration.md](configuration.md) and
`SECURITY.md`, which also explains why a single-target build has no
equivalent metric: it has no reload at all.
